<rml>
<head>
	<title>Main Frame</title>
	<link type="text/rcss" href="base.rcss"/>
	<link type="text/rcss" href="main_frame.rcss"/>
</head>
<body data-model="main_frame">
	<!-- Phase 1 of 3 of the third CNewUIObj-tier pilot -- HP/MP/AG/SD/EXP bars + 5 corner
	     buttons only. The skill hotkey row/grid and QWER item hotkeys are NOT here -- they stay
	     fully legacy-rendered (CNewUISkillList/CNewUIItemHotKey), including the center/left band
	     background chrome they still share pixels with (RenderLeftFrame()/RenderCenterFrame() in
	     NewUIMainFrameWindow.cpp, kept legacy since RmlUi always paints last in the frame -- see
	     that file's own header comment for the full reasoning; the item-hotkey band itself now
	     also reuses the SAME transform as this whole group, per feedback -- see Render()'s own
	     comment for that change).

	     #bars/#buttons/#exp are ONE shared transform group -- #bars binds left/top/scale live
	     from UI::Scaling::BottomHudCenterTransform (SyncRmlModel()) so the whole group tracks the
	     still-legacy center-band chrome's window-size-driven scale exactly (a CCharInfoBalloon-
	     style computed-position carve-out from this branch's usual fixed-dp-anchor policy --
	     see MainFrameRmlModel::barsLeft's header comment for the full history of why buttons/exp
	     ended up folded into this same group rather than using their own independent transforms).
	     #buttons and #exp are nested INSIDE #bars specifically so they inherit that one bound
	     transform for free -- every element below, at every nesting depth, uses static, UN-
	     rebased *reference-pixel* coordinates taken directly from the legacy render calls' own
	     constants (RenderLifeMana()/RenderGuageAG()/RenderGuageSD()/SetButtonInfo()/
	     RenderExperience()); the one transform on #bars alone maps this entire local layout onto
	     real window pixels. -->
	<div id="bars" data-style-left="bars_left + 'px'" data-style-top="bars_top + 'px'" data-style-transform="'scale(' + bars_scale + ')'">
		<!-- 2026-09-02: these two are read-only-from-C++ layout anchors (.layout-anchor, base.rcss),
		     not visuals -- they let each theme's own RCSS say where it wants the still-legacy
		     item-hotkey (potion) band and skill-hotkey band anchored, without any C++ knowing which
		     theme is active. NewUIMainFrameWindow.cpp's SyncRmlModel() reads each marker's real
		     screen position (Element::GetAbsoluteOffset()) every frame and turns it into an offset
		     applied to the transform CNewUIItemHotKey/CNewUISkillList's own still-legacy render and
		     click-hit-testing use -- see that function's own comment for the full mechanism, and
		     architecture-principles.md §16/§18/§19/§23 for why this exists instead of a per-theme
		     C++ branch. Children of #bars, same as everything else in this group, so their `left`/
		     `top` are plain local reference-pixel values riding the same bound transform. Legacy's
		     own main_frame.rcss positions both at the origin (0,0) -- zero offset, so legacy's
		     already-correct band positions are completely unaffected by this mechanism existing. -->
		<div id="item_hotkey_anchor" class="layout-anchor"></div>
		<div id="skill_list_anchor" class="layout-anchor"></div>
		<!-- .slot-frame/.slot-fill: shared unified visual identity for every "slot-like" box in
		     this group (gauges, buttons, and the decorative item/skill outlines further down) --
		     modern theme only (feedback: "unified styling ... if in the future some modders make
		     a theme, they can style and anchor it however they want"). .slot-frame is the shared
		     border/radius; .slot-fill is a separate class (not everything gets it -- see
		     #item_slots/#skill_slots's own comment for why) so a single themes/modern/
		     main_frame.rcess rule reskins every slot at once. Legacy ignores both classes (its
		     own id/class rules style these elements directly, unaffected by an extra unused
		     class name). -->
		<!-- 2026-09-01: tooltip spans used to live NESTED inside their trigger (.gauge-slot/
		     .frame-btn below) -- moved to be SIBLINGS of their trigger instead (still immediately
		     after it, for readability), shown via the RCSS `~` general-sibling combinator
		     (`#bar_hp:hover ~ .tooltip-hp`, see main_frame.rcss) rather than a descendant selector.
		     This isn't cosmetic: RmlUi's shrink-to-fit width for an absolutely positioned element
		     is hard-clamped to its CONTAINING BLOCK's width (LayoutDetails::GetShrinkToFitWidth,
		     `shrink_to_fit_width = Math::Min(shrink_to_fit_width, available_width)` --
		     ThirdParty/RmlUi/Source/Core/Layout/LayoutDetails.cpp) -- with the tooltip nested
		     inside a 45px-or-narrower gauge/button, THAT narrow box is its containing block, so the
		     tooltip's own background could never grow past it no matter how long the text was
		     (feedback: "the tooltip fill seems to take the size relative to the button ... we need
		     to make it fill the whole text"). Moved out here, the tooltip's containing block
		     becomes #bars itself, which main_frame.rcss now gives an explicit generous width for
		     exactly this reason. Each tooltip needs its own explicit `left` now instead of the old
		     `left:50%` (that 50% used to resolve against the narrow trigger's own width to center
		     over it; against #bars's own much wider width it would land in the wrong place) --
		     see main_frame.rcss's .tooltip-hp/sd/ag/mp rules. -->
		<!-- 2026-09-02: each gauge now carries TWO fill divs, not one -- .gauge-fill-vertical (the
		     original bottom-up fill, data-style-height) and .gauge-fill-horizontal (a new left-to-
		     right fill, data-style-width), both bound to the SAME fraction. Only one is ever
		     visible per theme (legacy hides .gauge-fill-horizontal, modern hides
		     .gauge-fill-vertical -- see each theme's own main_frame.rcss) -- needed because
		     data-style-* sets an INLINE style, which beats any stylesheet rule, so a single fill
		     div can't be height-bound for legacy and width-bound for modern at once; two divs, one
		     hidden per theme, is the same pattern already used for .btn-icon-label/
		     .slot-hotkey-label elsewhere in this file. Modern moved its 4 gauges to a horizontal-
		     bar mini-panel above the potion/skill row instead of vertical bars squeezed between
		     them (feedback: "removing it from the same row as the skills and potions icon and make
		     a horizontal progress bar on top of it") -- see themes/modern/main_frame.rcss's own
		     comment on #bar_hp for the panel layout. Legacy's own layout/positions are completely
		     unchanged; this is a modern-only redesign expressed entirely in CSS plus this one
		     additive markup change. -->
		<!-- 2026-09-02 redesign #8 (feedback: "the gauge bars ... would benefit from a dark
		     background (same as the potions and skills) the background should align with the exp
		     bar"): a background-only panel per stack, sized to the FULL row height (matching
		     potions/skills' own legacy-drawn chrome panel height exactly, touching the exp bar
		     below with no gap) rather than each individual bar's own small slot-fill box floating
		     mid-row. Placed before #bar_hp/etc in the DOM specifically so they paint first,
		     underneath the actual bars (same-stacking-level siblings paint in DOM order) -- purely
		     decorative, pointer-events:none, no data bindings. Modern-only (see that file's own
		     comment); legacy hides them outright, its own bars already sit against real sprite/
		     texture art with no gap to fill. -->
		<div id="gauge_bg_left" class="slot-frame slot-fill"></div>
		<div id="gauge_bg_right" class="slot-frame slot-fill"></div>
		<div id="bar_hp" class="gauge-slot slot-frame slot-fill">
			<div id="bar_hp_fill" class="gauge-fill gauge-fill-hp gauge-fill-vertical" data-class-poisoned="poisoned" data-style-height="hp_fraction * 100 + '%'"></div>
			<div class="gauge-fill gauge-fill-hp gauge-fill-horizontal" data-class-poisoned="poisoned" data-style-width="hp_fraction * 100 + '%'"></div>
			<span class="gauge-num">{{hp_text}}</span>
		</div>
		<span class="tooltip tooltip-hp">{{hp_tooltip}}</span>
		<div id="bar_sd" class="gauge-slot slot-frame slot-fill">
			<div class="gauge-fill gauge-fill-sd gauge-fill-vertical" data-style-height="sd_fraction * 100 + '%'"></div>
			<div class="gauge-fill gauge-fill-sd gauge-fill-horizontal" data-style-width="sd_fraction * 100 + '%'"></div>
			<span class="gauge-num">{{sd_text}}</span>
		</div>
		<span class="tooltip tooltip-sd">{{sd_tooltip}}</span>
		<div id="bar_ag" class="gauge-slot slot-frame slot-fill">
			<div class="gauge-fill gauge-fill-ag gauge-fill-vertical" data-style-height="ag_fraction * 100 + '%'"></div>
			<div class="gauge-fill gauge-fill-ag gauge-fill-horizontal" data-style-width="ag_fraction * 100 + '%'"></div>
			<span class="gauge-num">{{ag_text}}</span>
		</div>
		<span class="tooltip tooltip-ag">{{ag_tooltip}}</span>
		<div id="bar_mp" class="gauge-slot slot-frame slot-fill">
			<div class="gauge-fill gauge-fill-mp gauge-fill-vertical" data-style-height="mp_fraction * 100 + '%'"></div>
			<div class="gauge-fill gauge-fill-mp gauge-fill-horizontal" data-style-width="mp_fraction * 100 + '%'"></div>
			<span class="gauge-num">{{mp_text}}</span>
		</div>
		<span class="tooltip tooltip-mp">{{mp_tooltip}}</span>

		<!-- Buttons: 2026-09-02 redesign #5 moved these out of the main row entirely, into the band
		     above it, split left/right above their matching gauge stack -- see themes/modern/
		     main_frame.rcss's own #buttons comment for the row math. Reusing this group's
		     transform (not an independent one) is still what keeps them tracking the still-legacy
		     center-band chrome's own window-size-driven scale, same reasoning as always. -->
		<!-- .btn-icon-label spans: legacy hides these (its sprite art already has an icon baked in),
		     modern shows them -- was a single-letter monogram, now a short word ("SHOP"/"CHAR"/
		     "INV"/"FRIEND"/"MENU") per feedback ("words as label instead of shortcuts"). Same
		     display:none/shown split as login_main.rml's M/C buttons (see themes/modern/
		     main_frame.rcss's header comment). -->
		<!-- Same tooltip-as-sibling-not-descendant restructure as the gauges above (see that
		     comment) -- #buttons is the tooltips' new containing block here instead of #bars, same
		     reasoning, own explicit width in main_frame.rcss. -->
		<div id="buttons">
			<div id="btn_cshop" class="frame-btn slot-frame slot-fill" data-class-open="cshop_open" data-event-click="mainframe_cshop_click">
				<span class="btn-icon-label">SHOP</span>
			</div>
			<span class="tooltip tooltip-cshop">{{cshop_tooltip}}</span>
			<div id="btn_chainfo" class="frame-btn slot-frame slot-fill" data-class-open="chainfo_open" data-event-click="mainframe_chainfo_click">
				<span class="btn-icon-label">CHAR</span>
				<div class="alert-dot" data-class-hidden="!chainfo_alert"></div>
			</div>
			<span class="tooltip tooltip-chainfo">{{chainfo_tooltip}}</span>
			<div id="btn_myinven" class="frame-btn slot-frame slot-fill" data-class-open="myinven_open" data-event-click="mainframe_myinven_click">
				<span class="btn-icon-label">INV</span>
			</div>
			<span class="tooltip tooltip-myinven">{{myinven_tooltip}}</span>
			<div id="btn_friend" class="frame-btn slot-frame slot-fill" data-class-open="friend_open" data-event-click="mainframe_friend_click">
				<span class="btn-icon-label">FRIEND</span>
				<div class="alert-dot" data-class-hidden="!friend_alert"></div>
			</div>
			<span class="tooltip tooltip-friend">{{friend_tooltip}}</span>
			<div id="btn_window" class="frame-btn slot-frame slot-fill" data-class-open="window_open" data-event-click="mainframe_window_click">
				<span class="btn-icon-label">MENU</span>
			</div>
			<span class="tooltip tooltip-window">{{window_tooltip}}</span>
		</div>

		<!-- EXP: per feedback, spans from the item-hotkey band's own start (reference x=0) to the
		     button group's own end (reference x=640) -- i.e. exactly this shared group's own
		     640-reference-unit width, not the legacy EXP band's real full-window-width span.
		     expFraction is progress *within* the current decile, not overall level progress --
		     see NewUIMainFrameWindow.cpp's SyncRmlModel() comment. -->
		<div id="exp">
			<div id="exp_track" class="slot-frame slot-fill">
				<div id="exp_fill" data-style-width="exp_fraction * 100 + '%'"></div>
			</div>
			<span id="exp_digit">{{exp_digit}}</span>
			<span class="tooltip" id="exp_tooltip">{{exp_tooltip}}</span>
		</div>

		<!-- Decorative slot outlines for the still-legacy item-hotkey (potion) and skill icons --
		     modern theme only (legacy hides #item_slots/#skill_slots entirely; its own sprite
		     chrome already frames these slots, see themes/legacy/main_frame.rcss's rule).
		     .slot-frame only, deliberately NOT .slot-fill (unlike the gauges/buttons above):
		     RmlUi paints its whole document LAST in the frame (after the legacy 2D chrome AND
		     the 3D-composited item/skill icons -- see this file's own header comment and
		     NewUIMainFrameWindow.cpp's Render3D()), so a *filled* box here would paint over and
		     hide those icons; a border-only frame sized to surround, not overlap, each icon's
		     own fixed reference-pixel rect (CNewUIItemHotKey::RenderItems()/
		     RenderCurrentSkillAndHotSkillList()'s own x/y/width/height constants, each padded a
		     few px) reads as a slot container without ever touching an icon pixel
		     (pointer-events:none too -- purely decorative, legacy click handling for these is
		     untouched). Same "plain static reference-pixel coordinates, inherits #bars's
		     transform" convention as every other element in this group. -->
		<!-- .slot-hotkey-label spans: legacy's own art (IMAGE_MENU_1, the left-band sprite
		     RenderLeftFrame() still draws for legacy theme) already has "Q/W/E/R" baked in above
		     each potion slot, so legacy hides these; modern's flat-quad replacement for that band
		     (see NewUIMainFrameWindow.cpp's RenderLeftFrame() comment) has no such labels, and the
		     3D-rendered potion icon itself sits bottom-anchored within its slot (not this outline's
		     doing -- CNewUIItemHotKey::RenderItems()'s own fixed render rect, out of this pilot's
		     scope), which read as unbalanced top padding without them (feedback: "not centered ...
		     but this might be expected ... we can add this in the upper left corner ... to make
		     the upper padding look intentional"). Same display:none(legacy)/shown(modern) split as
		     .btn-icon-label above. HOTKEY_Q/W/E/R = 0/1/2/3 (NewUIMainFrameWindow.h), matching
		     item_slot_0..3 left-to-right. -->
		<div id="item_slots">
			<div id="item_slot_0" class="slot-frame"><span class="slot-hotkey-label">Q</span></div>
			<div id="item_slot_1" class="slot-frame"><span class="slot-hotkey-label">W</span></div>
			<div id="item_slot_2" class="slot-frame"><span class="slot-hotkey-label">E</span></div>
			<div id="item_slot_3" class="slot-frame"><span class="slot-hotkey-label">R</span></div>
		</div>
		<!-- data-class-selected: mirrors CNewUISkillList::IsHotKeySlotCurrentSkill(i) every frame
		     (SyncRmlModel()) -- modern theme highlights whichever slot currently shows the
		     equipped/active skill with a plain CSS class instead of the legacy IMAGE_SKILLBOX_USE
		     sprite that RenderCurrentSkillAndHotSkillList() used to always draw there (feedback:
		     "seems to use the legacy sprite outline ... change this to just programmatic outline
		     to highlight the selected skill"). Legacy theme ignores the class -- its own sprite,
		     still drawn as before, already provides that look. -->
		<div id="skill_slots">
			<div id="skill_slot_0" class="slot-frame" data-class-selected="skill_slot_0_selected"></div>
			<div id="skill_slot_1" class="slot-frame" data-class-selected="skill_slot_1_selected"></div>
			<div id="skill_slot_2" class="slot-frame" data-class-selected="skill_slot_2_selected"></div>
			<div id="skill_slot_3" class="slot-frame" data-class-selected="skill_slot_3_selected"></div>
			<div id="skill_slot_4" class="slot-frame" data-class-selected="skill_slot_4_selected"></div>
			<div id="current_skill_slot" class="slot-frame"></div>
		</div>
	</div>

	<!-- 2026-09-02 redesign #7 (feedback: "these shop/char/inv/friend/menu be relocated at the top
	     -- same concept as the mu helper bar, but not IN the mu helper bar ... the top HUD, at
	     least visually, will contain the hero position + helper buttons in the left corner, and
	     upper right area will contain the system buttons"): a SECOND, independent copy of the 5
	     buttons, sibling of #bars rather than nested inside it -- .anchor-top-right (base.rcss),
	     fixed dp size, genuinely independent of the bottom HUD's bars_scale/BottomHudScale system
	     entirely (this branch's NORMAL fixed-dp-anchor policy, not the bottom-HUD carve-out --
	     see layout-and-scaling.md). Deliberately NOT touching mu_helper_bar.rml/.rcss at all --
	     this is its own sibling element, visually echoing that panel's own #panel styling
	     (themes/modern/mu_helper_bar.rcss) for a cohesive top-HUD feel, not a merge into it.
	     Modern-only (legacy keeps its original bottom-HUD button row, sees #buttons_top and hides
	     it entirely -- themes/legacy/main_frame.rcss's own rule); modern hides nothing extra
	     here, #buttons is display:none'd for modern above. Same data-event-click
	     bindings as the originals -- RmlUi doesn't care how many elements trigger the same C++
	     callback, and only one copy is ever visible (display:none) per theme, so there's no
	     double-fire risk. Same data-class-open/data-class-hidden bindings too, so the "currently
	     open" highlight and alert dots stay in sync regardless of which copy is visible. -->
	<div id="buttons_top" class="anchor-top-right">
		<div id="btn_cshop_top" class="frame-btn-top" data-class-open="cshop_open" data-event-click="mainframe_cshop_click">
			<span>SHOP</span>
		</div>
		<span class="tooltip-top" id="tooltip_cshop_top">{{cshop_tooltip}}</span>
		<div id="btn_chainfo_top" class="frame-btn-top" data-class-open="chainfo_open" data-event-click="mainframe_chainfo_click">
			<span>CHAR</span>
			<div class="alert-dot-top" data-class-hidden="!chainfo_alert"></div>
		</div>
		<span class="tooltip-top" id="tooltip_chainfo_top">{{chainfo_tooltip}}</span>
		<div id="btn_myinven_top" class="frame-btn-top" data-class-open="myinven_open" data-event-click="mainframe_myinven_click">
			<span>INV</span>
		</div>
		<span class="tooltip-top" id="tooltip_myinven_top">{{myinven_tooltip}}</span>
		<div id="btn_friend_top" class="frame-btn-top" data-class-open="friend_open" data-event-click="mainframe_friend_click">
			<span>FRIEND</span>
			<div class="alert-dot-top" data-class-hidden="!friend_alert"></div>
		</div>
		<span class="tooltip-top" id="tooltip_friend_top">{{friend_tooltip}}</span>
		<div id="btn_window_top" class="frame-btn-top" data-class-open="window_open" data-event-click="mainframe_window_click">
			<span>MENU</span>
		</div>
		<span class="tooltip-top" id="tooltip_window_top">{{window_tooltip}}</span>
	</div>
</body>
</rml>
